<p>One of the most useful questions before buying is not “what is the best Wagyu?” but <strong>“which Wagyu cut suits the experience I want?”</strong> A4 or A5 grade and BMS provide valuable quality information, but they do not erase anatomical differences. Muscle location still shapes texture, flavour, fat distribution and cooking behaviour.</p>
<p>Two Japanese Wagyu products with the same grade can therefore eat very differently. One may be intensely melting, another more delicate, while another retains a meatier structure that works better in a larger portion.</p>
<h2>Start with how you want to eat the Wagyu</h2>
<p>Common scenarios include a few-bite tasting, a multi-course dinner with Wagyu as the centrepiece, a more steak-like meal, or a side-by-side comparison of several cuts. The more marbled the meat, the more important portion size becomes.</p>
<h2>Ribeye and upper loin: for marbling impact and flavour</h2>
<p>Upper-loin cuts are often prized for their combination of <strong>marbling, tenderness and flavour</strong>. Naming conventions vary between Japan, Spain and English-speaking cutting systems, so read the seller’s anatomical description rather than relying on one translated name.</p>
<p>Highly marbled A5 from this area can deliver the visual and sensory effect many people associate with Japanese Wagyu: fine intramuscular fat, remarkable juiciness and a melting texture.</p>
<h2>Striploin: balance, structure and marbling</h2>
<p>Striploin combines tenderness with a somewhat more defined structure. It can still carry spectacular marbling, but the fat distribution and mouthfeel differ from other loin cuts.</p>
<p>It can suit diners who want the meat itself to remain clearly present while still experiencing rich Wagyu fat.</p>
<h2>Tenderloin: when tenderness is the priority</h2>
<p>Tenderloin remains one of the most tender muscles in the animal. But <strong>tenderness and maximum marbling are not the same thing</strong>. Tenderloin may show a different marbling pattern from cuts more famous for dramatic fat veining.</p>
<p>Choose it for delicate texture rather than assuming that its prestige automatically means the most extreme marbling.</p>
<h2>Picanha: a meatier cut with its own fat profile</h2>
<p>Picanha comes from the rump area and is known for flavourful meat and a characteristic fat cap. In Wagyu it can combine that identity with intramuscular marbling, giving a meatier sense of structure without losing richness.</p>
<h2>Other cuts deserve attention</h2>
<p>Chuck roll, skirt, flank, rump and other cuts may appear in Japanese Wagyu catalogues depending on the importer’s cutting system. Some are more fibrous or connective, others carry different fat patterns.</p>
<p>A less famous cut is not automatically a lesser gastronomic product. It can reveal how Wagyu characteristics express themselves across the carcass.</p>
<h2>For a first tasting, what should you prioritise?</h2>
<p>Look for clear origin, good traceability, understandable grading and a cut with enough marbling to demonstrate why Japanese Wagyu is distinctive. You do not need the highest BMS or most expensive regional name.</p>
<p>A small quantity of a recognisable cut may teach you more than an oversized steak. If several cuts are available in tasting portions, comparison can be especially informative.</p>
<h2>If you want the most melting experience</h2>
<p>Look for abundant, fine marbling. Loin cuts are commonly strong candidates. With very high-BMS A5, richness can be intense, so smaller pieces and controlled heat make sense.</p>
<h2>If you want more beef flavour and less richness</h2>
<p>You can choose a more structured cut, a less extreme BMS or a well-documented A4. That is not “lowering quality”; it is choosing a different sensory balance.</p>
<h2>If you plan to grill it</h2>
<p>Grilling can work, but highly marbled fat dripping onto flames may cause flare-ups and bitter flavours. More structured cuts can be easier to manage than thin slices of extremely marbled meat. A pan or griddle offers excellent control for a first attempt.</p>
<h2>How to compare two cuts before buying</h2>
<ul>
<li>origin and traceability;</li>
<li>grade and BMS where available;</li>
<li>anatomical cut;</li>
<li>thickness and weight;</li>
<li>intramuscular and external fat;</li>
<li>planned cooking method;</li>
<li>number of diners;</li>
<li>price per serving, not only per kilogram.</li>
</ul>
<h2>So what is the best Wagyu cut?</h2>
<p>There is no single useful answer. <strong>The best cut is the one that matches the texture, richness, quantity and cooking style you want.</strong> Ribeye can showcase marbling, striploin offers another balance, tenderloin emphasises tenderness, and picanha or other cuts can provide a meatier character.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Is ribeye always the most marbled cut?</h3>
<p>Not necessarily in every carcass, although it is strongly associated with marbling. The actual piece and its grading data matter.</p>
<h3>Is Wagyu tenderloin the best cut?</h3>
<p>It is exceptionally tender, but the best choice depends on whether you prioritise tenderness, marbling, flavour or balance.</p>
<h3>Are less famous Wagyu cuts worth buying?</h3>
<p>Yes. Well-identified and traceable cuts can offer excellent flavour and reveal a different side of the animal.</p>
<h3>Does BMS matter when choosing a cut?</h3>
<p>Yes, but it should be considered together with anatomy, serving size and cooking method.</p>
